  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1111 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThe pattern begins to shift midweek as a weak surface low takes\nshape near the Arklatex and a residual boundary tries to lift\nnorth into the TN Valley. Moisture will slowly increase, and that\nis enough to reintroduce some rain chances. Wednesday looks \nmostly sunny early, then partly cloudy as the day progresses. A \nslight chance of showers and a few thunderstorms develops during \nthe afternoon and evening, mainly as weak shortwave energy and the\napproaching boundary provide just enough lift. Highs climb into \nthe lower 90s. Coverage is not expected to be widespread, but \nenvironmental parameters are just marginal enough that a few \nstrong storms could be possible. Wednesday night keeps low chances\nfor showers and overnight lows staying in the 70s. Thursday \nstarts with lingering low chances in the morning that gradually \ndiminish. A slight chance of afternoon storms remain possible \nbefore the system shifts east and drier air begins to filter back \nin. Highs stay in the lower 90s. From Friday through the weekend \nthe forecast settles back into a quieter regime. Mostly sunny to \nclear skies, dominate, with highs in the lower 90s and overnight \nlows in the upper 60s to around 70. Humidity recovers a bit but \nstays manageable. No significant weather is expected. \n\nLooking into early next week, temperatures trend a little warmer,\npossibly brushing the mid 90s in spots by Monday. The overall\npattern still looks relatively quiet.",
